------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase Q: how far does the core run ahead of the Tensix backend?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXPLORATORY. Nothing in the ISA documentation or either vendor tree gives
  a Tensix instruction queue depth, so there is no prediction here to
  confirm or refute -- only a number nothing has measured.

  CYCLES BELOW ARE RAW and every derived number is a DIFFERENCE of two of
  them, over the widest span available. `q_ctrl` is NOT subtracted point by
  point: the cascade evaluates all seven of its `if (p >= k)` tests at EVERY
  burst length, so its cost is constant in n rather than growing with it, and
  what does vary -- branch directions, first touch of the deeper blocks -- is
  non-monotonic. It is the noise floor, printed as such.

  TWO BURST FORMS, because the burst had to get longer and a longer
  straight-line burst is a longer INSTRUCTION STREAM:
    cascade  n = 1..128     2^p copies emitted straight-line. At n = 1024
                            this would be 4 KiB of text, in the octave where
                            phase F found a fetch cliff -- and a fetch cost
                            that grows with n is indistinguishable from
                            back-pressure, which is what this phase reads.
                            So the cascade was NOT extended.
    loop     n = 16..1024   n/16 iterations of one 16-instruction block: 64
                            bytes of text at every n, so no difference
                            between two loop points can be instruction
                            fetch. `q_loop_addi` is the same loop with
                            `addi` bodies and measures what the form itself
                            costs, back edge included.
  The forms OVERLAP at n = 16..128 and the overlap is checked below: it is
  the evidence that changing the form did not change the quantity.

  [t1 thread 1]
    cascade, raw cycles         1      2      4      8     16     32     64    128      rate
    q_ctrl                     11      7     16     11     24     27     28     22   (noise)
    q_nop                       5      9     22     34     46     55    103    188     1.268
    q_setdmareg                 5      9     28     29     45     67    101    180     1.205
    q_adddmareg                 5      9     22     36     47     61    111    308     2.330
    q_adddmareg_sync           19     18     28     36     61    109    205    396     2.991
    loop, raw cycles           16     32     64    128    256    512   1024      rate
    q_loop_addi                35     50     86    158    302    590   1166     1.122
    q_loop_adddmareg           35     44    117    309    693   1461   2997     2.938
    q_loop_adddmareg_sync      70    110    206    398    782   1550   3086     2.992
    noise floor: `q_ctrl` spans 7-28 cycles (21) across the burst lengths;
      that is what one raw point can be wrong by.
    form check, ADDDMAREG burst, cascade vs loop where both run:
             n    cascade       loop  loop-casc
            16         47         35        -12
            32         61         44        -17
            64        111        117         +6
           128        308        309         +1
      -> over n=16..128: cascade 2.330, loop 2.446 cycles per instruction
         (difference +0.116 -- the loop's own back edge is in this, and
         `q_loop_addi` above says how much of it that is)
    out to n=1024:  issuing core 2.938  |  drained 2.992  |  issue-limited baseline 1.122 (`q_loop_addi`)
    backlog in flight when the last .ttinsn returned (cycles of ThCon
    work, less 35 for tensix_sync()'s own cost at n=16):
      n=16 +0  n=32 +31  n=64 +54  n=128 +54  n=256 +54  n=512 +54  n=1024 +54  
    marginal cost between adjacent burst lengths:  16->32 0.56  32->64 2.28  64->128 3.00  128->256 3.00  256->512 3.00  512->1024 3.00  
    -> KNEE between n=64 and n=128: the marginal reaches 3.00 against a
       drained 2.992, so the core is back-pressured from there on.
    -> the backlog FLATTENED at ~54 cycles (last two doublings added
       0 then 0), clearing a 42-cycle noise floor, and at the
       drained rate of 2.992 cycles per instruction that is ~18
       INSTRUCTIONS in flight -- a measurement of the Tensix
       instruction queue's depth in entries.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
phase S: is the Tensix instruction queue shared, or one per thread?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  EXPLORATORY, and it is the question phase Q left open: that phase resolves
  a depth at ONE issuing thread and cannot resolve one at any other, and a
  shared queue of depth D looks exactly like a per-thread queue of depth D
  when only one thread is looking at it. (Phase Q's own read-out prints a
  LOWER BOUND -- ~16 entries pre-drain, ~22 after QLOOPPROBE gained its
  untimed sync -- because it drops the term below; the settled figure on
  Blackhole silicon is ~31-32.)

  WHAT SEPARATES THE TWO, and what does not:
    * a second thread that only SPINS does not. It pushes nothing, so it
      holds no entry either way. It is run below anyway, as `s_solo_*`, and
      it is the control for `another core is awake` as against `another core
      is issuing` -- its depth must not move under EITHER hypothesis.
    * a second thread issuing at a low, known rate does not either: queue
      occupancy is arrival rate times residence time, so a thread served
      faster than it arrives holds ~0 entries however long it runs.
    * only a SATURATED second thread holds entries, and the backend
      bandwidth it necessarily steals is measured here (`s_co_sync`) and
      divided back out. That is `s_co_*`, and it is the discriminator.

  THE REFERENCE BURST IS n=4, not phase Q's 16, and that is the other half
  of why this can answer what phase Q could not. The backlog is
  (sync[n] - plain[n]) - (sync[ref] - plain[ref]), and the subtrahend is only
  `tensix_sync()`'s own cost if the queue is EMPTY at the reference burst. At
  n=16 it is not -- and at two or three issuing threads a shared queue's
  per-thread share may be smaller than the ~10 entries an n=16 burst leaves
  outstanding, which makes phase Q's multi-thread backlogs structurally zero
  rather than merely small.

  So the depth in ENTRIES reported below is
      D = backlog / S + 4 * (1 - p/S)
  with S the drained rate from the `_sync` probe and p the issue-limited
  rate from `s_loop_addi`. Both are measured in the same slot. The second
  term is the reference burst's own occupancy, which phase Q's read-out
  drops; it is ~2 entries here and ~10 there, so a phase-S depth at one
  thread is expected to come out ABOVE phase Q's printed figure by roughly
  that difference. If it does, phase Q's figure is a lower bound and this
  says by how much. If it does not, this correction is wrong and should be
  said so. On Blackhole silicon it did, and the two forms reconcile to 0.7
  entries once phase Q's bursts are drained too.

  [t1 thread 1]
    raw cycles             4       8      16      32      64     128     256     512      rate
    s_loop_addi           17      26      38      62     110     206     398     782     1.506
    s_co_plain            17      22      34      58     117     309     693    1461     2.843
    s_co_repeat           17      22      34      58     117     309     693    1461     2.843
    s_co_sync             29      38      62     110     206     398     782    1550     2.994
    s_solo_plain          14      24      36      60     117     309     693    1461     2.848
    s_solo_sync           24      38      62     110     206     398     782    1550     3.004
    repeatability: |s_co_plain - s_co_repeat| <= 0 cycles; a backlog must
      clear twice that before it is divided by anything.
    co-issuing          backlog 77 cycles (last doubling +0), drained 2.994,
      issue-limited 1.506  ->  DEPTH ~28 ENTRIES
    solo (others spin)  backlog 79 cycles (last doubling +0), drained 3.004,
      issue-limited 1.506  ->  DEPTH ~28 ENTRIES

  VERDICT (thread 1, the one every variant activates, so the only thing
  that changes between these rows is what the OTHER cores are doing):
    only one thread count was run (--variants), and this phase's answer is a
    comparison between thread counts. Run at least `--variants t1,t2`.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Did the new probes run at all? (a null is not an absence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  phase S probes:        s_loop_addi ran  s_co_plain ran  s_co_repeat ran  s_co_sync ran  s_solo_plain ran  s_solo_sync ran  
    The structural check for this phase is that `s_co_sync` exceeds
    `s_co_plain` at every burst length -- a drain cannot be free. If the
    two are equal the phase measured nothing whatever the verdict said.
    Against tt-sim the backlog grows without bound (its Tensix queue is a
    list append), so every slot refuses a depth and there is no verdict:
    forced, and a fact about the simulator rather than about any card.

